{"name":"Plutonium","hex":"#35fa00","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"plutonium","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The transuranic chemical element with atomic number 94 and symbol Pu: a silvery-gray fissile radioactive actinide metal that tarnishes when exposed to air.\n\n An atom of this element.","An atom of this element."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/plutonium"}],"entries":[{"title":"Plutonium","blocks":[{"text":"Plutonium is a chemical element; it has symbol Pu and atomic number 94. It is a silvery-gray actinide metal that tarnishes when exposed to air, and forms a dull coating when oxidized. The element normally exhibits six allotropes and four oxidation states. It reacts with carbon, halogens, nitrogen, silicon and hydrogen. When exposed to moist air, it forms oxides and hydrides that can expand the sample up to 70% in volume, which in turn flake off as a powder that is pyrophoric. It is radioactive and can accumulate in bones, which makes the handling of plutonium dangerous."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Plutonium_ring.jpg/330px-Plutonium_ring.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plutonium","wikidata":"Q1102"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Plutonium_in_solution.jpg/500px-Plutonium_in_solution.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Plutonium_in_solution.jpg/500px-Plutonium_in_solution.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Various oxidation states of plutonium in solution","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APlutonium_in_solution.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Plutonium_pyrophoricity.jpg/500px-Plutonium_pyrophoricity.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_